<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kutadgu Bilig</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1069</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kutadgu Bilig ('Wisdom of Royal Glory') is a long didactic poem in Karakhanid Turkic written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Balasaguni</a> and presented to the khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> around 1069. Framed as dialogues between a king, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and a sage, it is a mirror for princes on justice, statecraft and the good life, and one of the earliest major works of Turkic Islamic literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig.jpg" alt="Kutadgu Bilig" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QutadughuBiliq_wien_p.10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The author speaks of himself at several points in the poem. Named Yūsuf, he was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a>, then the winter capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, near present-day Tokmok in Kyrgyzstan, and was about 50 when he finished the work in 1069-1070. He presented it to Tavghach Bughra Khan, the prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, and was given the title Khāṣṣ Ḥājib, roughly Privy Chamberlain, by which he is often known. Some scholars suspect that the prologue, far more openly Islamic than the rest and partly in prose, is by another hand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem is built around four main characters, each standing, as the author says, for an abstract principle. Robert Dankoff suggests that Yūsuf sought to reconcile the urban Islamic and the nomadic Turkic traditions of wisdom among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, at a time when a move away from nomadic life had changed what was asked of a good ruler; Dankoff also sees some Greek and Buddhist influence.</p><p>The language is Karakhanid, a Karluk form of Middle Turkic close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a> but with many Persian words and calques from Arabic and Persian. Though it draws on the wisdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a> and the Qurʻān, it cites no Islamic text by name, which has been taken to support the view that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> through wandering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>.</p><p>Yūsuf wrote in the Arabic mutaqārib metre, in rhyming couplets of 11-syllable lines, the earliest known use of that metre in a Turkic language. As Turkic did not distinguish long and short vowels, he worked with open and closed syllables instead. The dialogue form has been compared with Turkic song contests such as the Kazakh and Kyrgyz aytış and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> askiya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The work was well known through the Timurid era, but only three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive, each named after the city where it was found: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Facsimiles of all three were printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1942 and 1943.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kutadgu Bilig” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yusuf Khass Hajib of Balasagun wrote the <b>Kutadgu Bilig</b> ('Wisdom of Royal Glory'), a mirror for princes in verse, around 1069–1070, and presented it to the khan at Kashgar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kutadgu Bilig</b>, “the wisdom that brings good fortune”, completed in 1069 by Yusuf Khass Hajib of Balasagun, is a long poem on statecraft and ethics in the form of dialogues between a ruler and his counsellors.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Kashgar became a centre of Turkic Islamic culture: Yusuf Balasaguni presented his mirror for princes, the <b>Kutadgu Bilig</b>, to the Kashgar khan around 1069.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-kara-khanid-khanate">Eastern Kara-Khanid Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
